okf_version: "1.0"
task: "Claude Code shows 'API error · Retrying in 0s · attempt 2/10' often (firstperson415, anthropic-routed, claude-opus-5-5)."
reported: "User, 2026-10-12 ~00:08, screenshot of Claude Code: 'API error · Retrying in 0s · attempt 2/10', status line '[claude-opus-5-5 (anthropic)] firstperson415 | ctx 615,811/1,000,000 tok (61.6%)', 'claude에서 api에러가 종종 자주나옴'."
observed:
  router: "Router 9469 (workspace 261058102223 = C:/Users/djlov/Documents/Unreal Projects/firstperson415), code 6638f33, one OAuth token in the pool (5h 47%, 7d 12%)."
  access_log: "router.log: 'POST /v1/messages' answered 500 at 23:58:57, 23:59:29, 00:00:01, 00:01:51, 00:01:52 (more at 00:07), each ~1 s after 'upstream_direct_request ... attempt=1/1 bytes=14.7-16.5 MB' (msgs 1174-1199, 77 tools). No WARN/ERROR line for these in router.log."
  events: "GET http://127.0.0.1:9469/ca/events/recent?level=error (in-memory event buffer): router.error 'upstream direct request failed provider=anthropic model=claude-opus-5-5: URLError: <urlopen error [WinError 10054] An existing connection was forcibly closed by the remote host>' (9x between 23:58:57 and 00:08:46) and once 'SSLError: [SSL: SSLV3_ALERT_BAD_RECORD_MAC] sslv3 alert bad record mac'; each followed by runtime.error HTTP 500."
  code_path: "upstream_retry.open_*: URLError/OSError that is not retried is re-raised as RuntimeError without router_log; claude_router.py:526-567 catches it, publishes router.error and writes a 500 api_error JSON to the client, again without router_log. HTTP errors from upstream are logged (upstream_direct_http_error)."
  size_vs_failure: "resets.py over today's router.log: 9469 requests >=10 MB: 11 of 43 answered 500; <1 MB: 0 of 2. 9611 (this ciel-runtime workspace) <1 MB: 5 of 130 answered 500, and its event buffer shows the same 10054 / BAD_RECORD_MAC errors (21:05, 23:48, 00:04, 00:07:36, 00:07:51)."
  same_minute: "00:07:07, 00:07:12, 00:07:29 (9469) and 00:07:36, 00:07:51 (9611): resets in two workspaces within one minute."
  claude_transcript: "Claude Code did not write today's retries into the session transcript (latest api_error entries there are ECONNRESET on 2026-09-21..28)."
not_known:
  - "Why api.anthropic.com connections are reset. Large requests fail far more often (11/43 at 15-16 MB vs 5/130 under 1 MB), and small requests in another workspace failed in the same minute; the network path is a possibility, not verified."
changes:
  - "User 2026-10-12: '아직 수정할사항잇으면 수정'. Only observability was changed (the cause is unknown; no retry or other behaviour change)."
  - "upstream_retry.open_provider_request_with_key_retry: a dropped connection / transport error that is not retried now writes a router activity error and WARN 'upstream_direct_transport_error ... error=... after=<s since the request was sent> tokens= bytes=' (same shape as the existing upstream_stream_transport_error); post_json_with_rate_retry logs 'upstream_transport_error'."
  - "claude_router: the catch-all that answers the client with a 500 api_error now logs ERROR 'anthropic_route_error ... status=500 error=...'."
verification:
  e2e: "evidence/e2e_dropped_connection.py: isolated router (own config dir, test key, router port) whose Anthropic base URL is a local fake upstream that reads the headers and part of a 2 MB body and closes with RST. Client gets the same 500 as in the live router ('ConnectionResetError: [WinError 10054] An existing connection was forcibly closed by the remote host'). router.log, previous code (0343d29): only 'upstream_direct_request' and 'access ... 500'. New code: plus 'upstream_direct_transport_error ... after=0.00s tokens=500027 bytes=2000109' and 'anthropic_route_error ... status=500' (evidence/result-ciel-runtime.json, result-head-0343d29.json)."
  tests: "router 1189 OK (new test_direct_anthropic_dropped_connection_is_logged), unit 1851 OK, runtime 405 OK, channel 457 OK; 9611 200."
next_evidence: "With the log line in place, router.log will show for each drop the request size and how long after sending it failed; that is what can tell request size apart from the network path."
